C('9-010',[18,19,20],'J156-9-03','6-109','sommets évanescents ... semi-évanescents','In a bounded ordered segment (I,ω0,ω1), the five-type polygon-vertex classification is modified at the ambient endpoints: an included endpoint adjacent to one interval behaves as semi-evanescent under complement, while an interior redundant vertex disappears permanently. Complement swaps isolated and lacunary vertices, preserves the nonambient boundary, removes evanescent vertices, and exchanges the two semi-evanescent endpoint classes. The text revises several decompositions before giving the disjoint decomposition of δΦ∪∂I on scan20; earlier formulas must not be flattened into one unchanged boundary convention.','corrected by himself later (both places)','intervalposets')
C('9-011',[20,21],'J156-9-03','113-145','cosupp°Φ=Omb°Φ*; Polyg(M)→Cons(M) bijective','For the bounded-segment interval magasin, the announced complement identity is Φ⊥=Omb°(Φ*), hence Φ⊥⊥=Omb°(Φ**). Constructible subsets are open shadows of finite polygons. A polygon is recoverable from its open shadow: take maximal interval elements and maximal point elements (isolated points), then add the included endpoints of the maximal intervals. Thus polygons and constructible subsets correspond bijectively, and perpendiculars of constructible subsets are constructible. The reconstruction is justified, while the main complement identity is stated without a separate full proof here.','proof sketched','intervalposets')
C('9-012',[21,22,23],'J156-9-03','147-195','At supp1; cardΦT=2n+1; 2^(2n+1)','Fix a finite flag T={ω0=t0<...<tn=ω1} containing both ambient endpoints. Let ΦT be its n interval strata and n+1 point strata. For every subset Φ⊆ΦT, its support is the double polar of Φ. The map P(ΦT)→Σ(M) preserves joins, complement, and hence meets, giving the local Boolean support axiom. It is asserted to give exactly 2^(2n+1) distinct supports subordinate to T. Finitely many mutually commensurable polygons admit a common such flag after subdivision; the written extension to arbitrary families needs the extra hypothesis of a common finite flag.','proof sketched','ortholattice')
C('9-013',[23,24,25,26,27],'J156-9-03','197-264','magasin résiduel ... J<a ... y compris si a minimal','The half-interval construction is replaced by residuals of a bounded ordered segment: delete either or both endpoint point strata while retaining all interval strata. On deleting the least endpoint, I_ω0,a becomes J_<a for EVERY a∈J, even minimal a; deleting both endpoints adds both half-interval families and a whole-interval symbol [J]. For J empty this still gives a singleton atelier of an indivisible open interval. Uniform data are (I,Ω0,Ω1), with Ω0,Ω1 either empty or singleton least/greatest endpoints, disjoint when both present. This explicitly supersedes the nonminimal/nonmaximal restrictions of scans9–16 and repairs the absence of a whole object.','corrected by himself later (both places)','intervalposets')
claims[-1]['lines'].append('J156-9-04:6-43')
C('9-014',[27,28],'J156-9-04','45-93','M(Φ) somme directe ... J somme lexicographique','The induced magasin on a constructible open shadow of a finite polygon Φ decomposes as the direct sum of those on its connected components. For a nonsingleton connected component, it is again an ordered-interval magasin whose underlying poset is the lexicographic concatenation of the naive interiors of its strata, with endpoints retained precisely when included in Φ. A displayed alternate formula for J is explicitly restricted by a difficult marginal qualification. The one-point component is treated separately.','stated without proof','intervalposets')
C('9-015',[28],'J156-9-04','87-93','Cas M divisible ... Mc est très dense','For a bounded segment, divisibility is expressed by order density: every a<b has c with a<c<b. Missing endpoint cases require appropriate unboundedness, and the biopen case also nonemptiness. The written direction of unboundedness for the one-endpoint cases appears reversed and is flagged. Under the intended divisibility condition, ordinary point and bounded-interval strata are claimed very dense among all generalized strata. The empty poset is excluded from the chosen definition of divisible poset although its one-stratum atelier is called divisible.','stated without proof','intervalposets')
C('9-016',[29,30],'J156-9-04','99-153','Somme directe de magasins; pas somme au sens catégorique','For a family of magasins Mi, form their disjoint union with incidence and open-refinement orders componentwise, and declare all strata from different components interior-disjoint. This satisfies the magasin axioms. Chosen figure collections Fi yield F={A: A∩Mi∈Fi for all i}, canonically the product of the Fi as ordered sets. It need not be a categorical coproduct for the then-current comorphisms, because of disjointness preservation. Decomposition by refinement-connected components requires the extra axiom Mag conn°: elements in different such components are disjoint.','proof sketched','magasins')
C('9-017',[31,32],'J156-9-04','159-204','SousMag ... Omb°(Φ) est un sous-magasin','An induced subset M′ of a magasin is a submagasin under a carrier-closure condition: for X≪°Y and X′ face of X, all three in M′, the unique carrier Y′ of X′ in Y must lie in M′. A stronger condition requiring every ambient refinement carrier between any X,Y∈M′ to lie in M′ is proposed in the margin, not imposed without qualification. For a prefigure Φ, its open shadow is proved to satisfy the stated weak condition by uniqueness of carriers in the face-closure of Φ.','proved there','magasins')
C('9-018',[32,33],'J156-9-04','206-262','Est-ce un sous-magasin? C’est faux en général','A constructible support or cosupport need not be a submagasin. In a triangle Y=abc with d interior to edge bc, take X=abd, X′=bd and its carrier Y′=bc. The point d is interior-disjoint from Y,X,X′, but not from Y′; hence the cosupport of {d} fails carrier closure. The proposed large residual {X:X⊥°Φ and X compatible with Φ} does satisfy carrier closure for a prefigure Φ: faces remain compatible, and a carrier belonging to Φ would contradict irreflexivity via open refinement.','proved there','magasins')
C('9-019',[34,35,36,37],'J156-9-05','14-118','compactologie ... Mag cpt1 ... plus grand atelier','A compactology Mc⊆M is extra data: compact strata have finite face sets; Mc is downward closed for refinement; each principal face set meets each compact test stratum in only finitely many non-wholly-disjoint strata (c). A subset Φ is locally finite iff for every X∈Mc only finitely many Y∈Φ fail whole-disjointness with X. Such subsets form an antifilter, and face-closure preserves local finiteness for prefigures. Locally finite figures form the largest compatible attached figure collection. Given an attached collection F, the strata meeting every F∈F in finitely many strata form the largest compatible compactology. Compact figures form an attached collection on Mc, generally not on all M.','proof sketched','compactology')
C('9-020',[34],'J156-9-05','31-44','compact ... Borel-Lebesgue ... riche','A compactological magasin is called compact when a finite figure has full support, equivalently its perpendicular is empty (the reading Mc versus M in the margin is uncertain). He proposes a further Borel–Lebesgue type axiom and richness under subdivisions, but neither is formulated. This is a definition plus a deferred programme, not a compactness theorem.','open on the pages','compactology')
C('9-021',[38,39,40,41],'J156-9-05','123-169','Dès maintenant ... atelier ... magasin ... inversement','The terminology is explicitly reversed: from scan41 onward, an atelier A is the former magasin, carrying incidence, open refinement, and symmetric irreflexive interior-disjointness; its strata are elements X∈A and its multistrata are principal face sets ↓X. Figures are actual subsets of A. A magasin is now an attached chosen collection of figures, with several possible magasins for one atelier. Compactology Ac⊆A is optional additional data, tentatively the heart. This reversal is essential for interpreting earlier and later claims consistently.','definition/construction','ateliers')
P('9-04','A stronger induced-subatelier condition',[{'cote':'156-9','scan':31,'file':'J156-9-04','lines':'165-169'}],'Il faudrait imposer une condition plus forte, à voir.','Compare closure of face-lifting diagrams with closure under every ambient refinement carrier; determine which notion preserves the intended induced refinement and compatibility relations.')
P('9-05','Borel–Lebesgue and richness axioms for compactology',[{'cote':'156-9','scan':34,'file':'J156-9-05','lines':'31-44'}],'Introduce Mag cpt2 of Borel–Lebesgue type and make compact strata rich under subdivisions.','Specify covers/subdivisions and a finite-subcover property, then prove that standard geometric interval/polyhedral examples satisfy it. The manuscript provides only the finite-intersection compactology conditions here.')
R('156-9',18,'J156-9-03:25','∂Φ∩Φint=∂I∩(Φ0∖Φis)','include both proper and improper internal endpoints','Improper ambient endpoints can be lacunary and hence internal without lying in Φ0. The adjacent marginal correction and later six-part decomposition supersede this formula.')
R('156-9',19,'J156-9-03:66','second and third terms exchange; third invariant','fourth term invariant','In δΦ=red∪is∪lac∪∂*Φ, the invariant term is ∂*Φ, not the third term already exchanged.')
R('156-9',23,'J156-9-03:185-194','famille quelconque ... un P(ΦT) convenable','finite family or common finite flag','Infinitely many polygons can involve infinitely many distinct vertices, so no finite T contains all vertices. The exact finite-cardinality formula applies only to finite T.')
R('156-9',28,'J156-9-04:91','(I,ω0) require b<a; (I,ω1) require b>a','reverse the directions for the one-endpoint cases','A least endpoint ω0 rules out a predecessor for a=ω0; the missing upper endpoint should require no greatest element. Dual contradiction for ω1.')
R('156-9',29,'J156-9-04:128','∀i∈Mi','∀i∈I','i is the family index; Mi is a component, not its index set.')
R('156-9',31,'J156-9-04:171','c’est une figure si M′ fermé par incidence','submagasin; figure only with pairwise compatibility','A downset need not be pairwise compatible. The parenthetic wording must be checked; it cannot assert that all downsets are figures.')
R('156-9',33,'J156-9-04:258','X,Y,X′∈M ⇒ Y′∈M','M′ in all four places','The displayed implication is otherwise tautological in the ambient magasin and does not express the required induced-submagasin condition.')
R('156-9',34,'J156-9-05:31','supp°F=Mc|M','M for claimed equivalence with empty cosupport','Full-support equivalence requires the top support M; equality to a proper compact subset would not imply empty perpendicular.')
R('156-9',34,'J156-9-05:44','Y parallel X finitely many','Y not parallel X finitely many','The formal definition on scan35 line58 uses non-whole-disjointness; using disjointness would reverse the intended compact test.')
